# Optional, defaults to "development" (Node's own default when unset). Set this to
# "production" in the real deployment - several things key off it: better-auth's rate limiter
# is only on by default in production (see RATE_LIMIT_* below), and its dev-mode IP fallback
# only kicks in when this is "development".
NODE_ENV=development

# It must be at least 32 characters and generated with high entropy.
# You can also use openssl rand -base64 32 to generate one.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# The PUBLIC url clients reach this service at - better-auth uses it for cookies, CSRF/origin
# checks and the JWKS issuer. Behind a load balancer this is e.g. https://staff.example.org
# (no port), and has no relation to the port the process listens on - see PORT below.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:5000

# Optional, defaults to 5000. The port this process binds to (see src/index.ts). Independent of
# BETTER_AUTH_URL: locally the two happen to match, in a deployment they don't.
PORT=5000

# Optional. Extra origins better-auth trusts for CSRF (the Origin header on POSTs) and for
# redirect targets (callbackURL, redirectTo), on top of BETTER_AUTH_URL's origin, which is always
# trusted. In production BETTER_AUTH_URL is staff-app's public URL, so leave this empty. Locally
# BETTER_AUTH_URL is this server (:5000) while the browser is on staff-app (:3001), hence the value
# below. Supports better-auth's patterns: "https://*.example.org", "myapp://" (mobile app).
TRUSTED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3001

# Optional. Origins allowed to call this server cross-origin from a browser (CORS). Empty means the
# CORS middleware isn't mounted at all, which is correct today, locally and in production: the
# browser reaches /api/auth/* through staff-app's same-origin proxy. Set it only for a browser app
# on another origin; native mobile apps don't use CORS. Exact origins only (no path or trailing slash).
CORS_ORIGINS=

# The single canonical origin staff-app is served from (no trailing slash needed - it's
# stripped defensively in src/lib/config.ts either way). Used to build absolute redirect
# URLs for the invite plugin (see src/lib/auth.ts) - an invitee's first click on an invite
# link lands cold on this server, with no staff-app page loaded yet to resolve a relative path.
STAFF_APP_URL=http://localhost:3001

DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/dbname

# All three below are optional - shown here at their defaults. Raise DB_POOL_MAX as concurrent
# traffic grows (keep it well under Postgres's max_connections, shared across all app instances).
# DB_CONNECTION_TIMEOUT_MS has no equivalent default in node-postgres itself (it defaults to no
# timeout, i.e. a hang) - we default it to 5s here so a dead/unreachable DB fails fast instead.
DB_POOL_MAX=10
DB_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
DB_CONNECTION_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# All three below are optional - shown here at their defaults. They bound the raw HTTP
# connection (separate from the DB timeouts above) so a slow/stalled client can't hold a
# connection open forever. HEADERS_TIMEOUT_MS must be <= REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS, and
# KEEP_ALIVE_TIMEOUT_MS must be < HEADERS_TIMEOUT_MS (validated at startup in src/lib/config.ts).
# Once this sits behind a reverse proxy/load balancer, raise KEEP_ALIVE_TIMEOUT_MS above that
# proxy's own idle timeout, or the proxy can try to reuse a connection this server just closed.
REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
HEADERS_TIMEOUT_MS=20000
KEEP_ALIVE_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Optional, defaults to 20000. On SIGTERM/SIGINT the server stops accepting connections, lets
# in-flight requests finish, closes the DB pool and exits (see src/lib/shutdown.ts). If that takes
# longer than this, the remaining connections are dropped and the process exits with code 1. Keep it
# below the orchestrator's grace period before SIGKILL: ECS stopTimeout is 30s by default, and
# Docker Compose's stop_grace_period is 10s by default, so raise it there.
SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS=20000

# Both optional - shown here at their defaults (also better-auth's own defaults). These set the
# *general* rate limit; better-auth already applies its own tighter built-in limits to
# sign-in/sign-up/password-reset regardless of these values, so no extra config is needed there.
# Whether rate limiting is enabled at all follows NODE_ENV above (on only in production).
RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=10
RATE_LIMIT_MAX=100

# Required: the variable must be present (startup fails if it's missing), but its value may be
# empty - so "no proxy in front" is always a deliberate choice, never a forgotten setting.
# Comma-separated IPs or CIDR ranges of the reverse proxies allowed to
# append to X-Forwarded-For (load balancer, CDN). better-auth keys rate limiting by client IP, read
# from that header: with a list set, it reads the header right to left, skips these proxies, and uses
# the first address that isn't one of them as the client, so a client-supplied leftmost entry is never
# believed. Leave empty when nothing trusted sits in front of this service (local dev, direct
# exposure): src/index.ts then overwrites the header with the real TCP peer address so a client can't
# spoof it to dodge rate limiting. Behind a proxy, leaving it empty puts every user in one shared
# bucket - e.g. in AWS set it to the VPC range, and add Cloudflare's ranges once it's proxying.
# Invalid entries fail startup; an empty value with NODE_ENV=production logs a startup warning.
TRUSTED_PROXIES=

RESEND_API_KEY=
RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=no-reply@example.com